## §30. Choreographic / multi-tier programming

This is one of the most underrated frontiers. Choreographic
programming (Carbone, Honda, Yoshida, ESOP 2007 "A Calculus of
Global Interaction"; Montesi's 2013 PhD; Cruz-Filipe & Montesi
2017–) lets you **write the global protocol** of a distributed system
in one program, and the compiler does **endpoint projection** to
derive the per-node code, with **deadlock-freedom-by-design**.

The reference points:
- **Pirouette** (Hirsch & Garg, POPL 2022) — higher-order functional
  choreographies; the message language is parameterized; deadlock
  freedom by construction, all verified in Coq. arXiv:2111.03484.
- **Choral** (Giallorenzo, Montesi, Peressotti, 2020) — Java-derived;
  choreographies as objects.
- **HasChor** (Shen, Kashiwa, Kuper, ICFP 2023) — Haskell library;
  "Functional Choreographic Programming for All."
- **Session types** (Honda, Yoshida, Carbone; multiparty session
  types JACM 2016) — the type-system tradition that choreographies
  subsume.

The closest non-academic precedents are:
- **Ur/Web** (Chlipala) — tier-less web programming.
- **Links** (Cooper, Lindley, Wadler, Yallop) — tier-less web with
  effects.
- **ScalaJS / Kotlin Multiplatform / F# Fable** — practical
  isomorphic dialects.

The convergence with §24: **choreographies are global effects.** A
"send X from Alice to Bob" is an effect that the choreographic type
system tracks. The endpoint projection is the same operation as
effect handler elaboration. A sovereign language with both algebraic
effects and choreographies has them as facets of one mechanism.

For SIGIL: this is the multi-tier story. The same source describes
the language fleet's interactions (OmniMindv2's components, the
human-AI partnership flow), and the compiler emits one component per
tier (server, client, GPU, NPU, edge device).

## §31. Logic programming, constraint logic programming, Datalog

The renaissance is real and worth understanding:
- **Souffle** (Scholz, Jordan, Subotic; CC 2016) — Datalog with C++
  backend; used in pointer analysis at Galois, Mozilla, GrammaTech.
  Production-grade.
- **Differential Datalog (DDlog)** (Ryzhyk, Budiu; VMware Research,
  since acquired) — incremental Datalog over differential dataflow.
  Used for OVN network configuration in VMware NSX.
- **Datafrog** (Niko Matsakis) — embedded Datalog in Rust; used in
  Polonius (Rust's next borrow checker).
- **Soufflé** continues to evolve.
- **Picat** (Zhou) — multiparadigm with logic, pattern matching,
  constraints, planning.
- **Mercury** (Somogyi, Henderson, Conway) — strongly typed logic +
  functional; high-performance.
- **Curry** (Hanus) — Haskell-syntax functional logic.
- **miniKanren** (Friedman, Byrd) — embeddable relational
  programming; remarkably small core.

The big idea: **relations are first-class alongside functions.** A
function is a relation that is total and right-unique. Datalog is a
sweet spot: it's decidable, polynomial-time, and expressive enough
for many "program analysis" tasks. Souffle's success in industrial
program analysis demonstrates this.

The convergence with §32 (dataflow): incremental Datalog
(Differential Datalog) is *implemented on* differential dataflow.
The same engine runs both.

## §32. Stream / dataflow programming

The mature lineage:
- **Lustre** (Caspi, Halbwachs, Pilaud, Plaice; POPL 1987) —
  synchronous dataflow; the language behind **SCADE Suite** which
  is used in Airbus avionics (DO-178C Level A certified). The
  language with arguably the highest stakes-per-line-of-code in
  production.
- **Esterel** (Berry; multiple TR/papers from 1992 onward) —
  synchronous concurrency; instants and signals; preemption
  primitives.
- **Signal** (Le Guernic, Gautier; INRIA) — relational synchronous
  language.
- **Reactive ML** (Mandel, Pouzet; PADL 2005) — OCaml + reactivity.
- **FRP** — Elliott's seminal work (Fran, 1997); Conal Elliott's
  "Genuinely Functional User Interfaces" (Haskell Workshop 2001);
  Reflex-FRP (Ryan Trinkle); Yampa; Sodium.

The frontier overlap with hardware:
- The AMD AIE-ML (Ryzen AI) is a 2D dataflow array — **the natural
  source language is a dataflow language.**
- Cerebras WSE is dataflow-coded via CSL.
- SambaNova RDU is dataflow-by-construction.
- Groq LPU is statically-scheduled dataflow.

A sovereign language with stream/dataflow as a first-class layer has
a *direct* path onto every modern accelerator that isn't a CUDA/GPU
clone. This is structurally adjacent to choreographic programming
(§30) — both describe global behavior in terms of communication.

## §33. Concatenative / point-free languages

Forth, Factor (Slava Pestov), Joy (Manfred von Thun), Cat
(Christopher Diggins), Kitten (Jon Purdy), Plorth, colorForth (Chuck
Moore). The stack-based paradigm has distinctive properties:

- **No register allocation in the traditional sense** — the stack
  *is* the register file conceptually; the compiler maps stack
  positions to registers.
- **Composition is concatenation** — `f g` is the function "do f
  then g." This is a categorical composition, structurally clean.
- **Stack-effect typing** — Factor's stack-effect types are
  essentially typed concatenation; Kitten has a more formal version.
- **Uiua** (Mortensen, 2023) brings stack-based + rank polymorphism +
  dense glyph — a 2020s synthesis.

For SIGIL: probably not as a primary surface, but **as one
projection** (per §13). The point-free form is natural for code that
an AI generates and a human reads at a glance.

## §34. Array languages taken seriously

Already touched on in §13 and §33. The serious reference points:
- **APL** (Iverson, Turing Award lecture "Notation as a Tool of
  Thought" 1979).
- **Remora** (Slepak, Shivers, Manolios; "An Array-Oriented Language
  with Static Rank Polymorphism," FLOPS 2014; "The Semantics of Rank
  Polymorphism," JFP, arXiv:1907.00509) — the *formal* semantics of
  rank-polymorphic typing.
- **Co-dfns** (Aaron Hsu) — APL compiler implemented in APL;
  demonstrates self-hosting of a heavily array-oriented language.
- **ArrayFire**, **Halide** (Ragan-Kelley et al., PLDI 2013) —
  schedule languages for array computation; Halide's separation of
  *algorithm* from *schedule* is a foundational idea.
- **Futhark** (Henriksen et al.) — purely functional array language,
  compiles to GPU.
- **Dex** (Maclaurin, Radul, Johnson et al., ICFP 2019 "Dex: array
  programming with typed indices") — typed indices and effect-free
  array computation.

Key takeaway: **rank polymorphism as a core type-system feature is
fully formalized** (Remora) and a sovereign language can adopt it.
The implementation cost is real but the design space is well-
explored.

## §35. Distributed-first semantics

The serious primitives:
- **CRDTs** (Shapiro, Preguiça, Baquero, Zawirski; "Conflict-Free
  Replicated Data Types," SSS 2011) — algebraic data types that
  converge under commutative merge. Implementations: Yjs, Automerge,
  Riak DT, Hypermerge.
- **CALM theorem** (Hellerstein, "The Declarative Imperative," PODS
  2010; Hellerstein-Alvaro, "Keeping CALM," CACM 2020) — **a
  distributed program produces deterministic output without
  coordination if and only if it is expressible in monotonic logic.**
  This is one of the deepest theoretical results in distributed
  computing.
- **Bloom / Bloom-L** (Conway, Marczak, Alvaro, Hellerstein, Maier) —
  language designs based on CALM.
- **Pact** (Kadena) — smart contract language with formal semantics;
  capability-based effects.
- **E** (Mark Miller, etc.; ERights.org) — capability-secure
  distributed language.
- **Pony's reference capabilities** — for distributed actors with
  safety guarantees.

The structural conclusion: **monotonicity is the type-system property
that buys you coordination-freedom.** A function that is provably
monotonic can be replicated and merged without coordination; one
that is not requires explicit coordination. A sovereign language can
track monotonicity in the type system (graded modal types, §26, are
the natural mechanism).

## §36. Capability-based programming

The reference points:
- **E** (Miller, Tribble, Shapiro; "Concurrency Among Strangers,"
  TGC 2005) — the modern object-capability language.
- **Pony** (Clebsch, Drossopoulou, et al.; OOPSLA 2013, 2015, 2017) —
  actor model + reference capabilities; production-grade for high-
  performance concurrent systems. Note the Pony community's
  clarification that Pony's *reference* capabilities are not Mark
  Miller's *object* capabilities, though Pony has both.
- **Monte** (Erights community continuation) — dynamic ocap language.
- **Joe-E** — Java subset enforcing ocap.
- **Cap'n Proto** — capability-secure RPC at the network layer.
- **Austral** (Borretti) — linear + capability-secure systems
  language.
- **Newspeak** — capability-secure platform.
- **Capsicum** — capability-secure OS sandboxing in FreeBSD.
- **WebAssembly Component Model / WASI Preview 2** — the new WASI
  uses capability-style imports rather than ambient authority. This
  is the most consequential mainstream adoption of ocap to date.

For SIGIL: **make capabilities the load-bearing security primitive.**
Every effect (per §24) requires a capability. The Prime Directive
then has a mechanical realization: surveillance is an effect that
requires a "read sensor" capability that consent rules govern;
coercion is an effect that requires a "constrain agent" capability
that the OS refuses to grant; manipulation requires a "modify agent
state" capability with consent scope.

## §37. Bisimulation and process calculi

The serious literature:
- **CSP** (Hoare; *Communicating Sequential Processes*, 1985) — the
  basis of occam, of FDR2/FDR3 model checking, conceptually of Go's
  channels.
- **CCS** (Milner; *Communication and Concurrency*, 1989) —
  bisimulation as the semantic equivalence.
- **π-calculus** (Milner, Parrow, Walker, Info. and Comp. 1992) —
  mobility; channel passing. Pict (Pierce, Turner) and JoCaml are
  direct implementations.
- **Join calculus** (Fournet, Gonthier, POPL 1996) — JoCaml.
- **Ambient calculus** (Cardelli, Gordon, FoSSaCS 1998) — locations
  and mobility.

The frontier inverts the usual stance: **what if concurrency is the
base layer and sequential execution is a special case?** This is
what HVM2 essentially does — its primitive is parallel rewriting of
an interaction net; sequential code is just a particular interaction-
net topology. Bend is the high-level surface; HVM2 is the runtime.

For SIGIL: the IR as a hypergraph (§14) with local rewrite rules is
*structurally identical* to a process calculus. The names align:
hyperedges = processes; node identities = channels; rewrites =
communications. The interaction-combinator model (HVM2) demonstrates
that this scales to 74,000 MIPS on a single GPU.

## §38. Self-adjusting / incremental computation

Already established in §27. The point worth surfacing: **incremental
computation, content-addressed code, and differential dataflow are
facets of one mechanism.**

- Content-addressed code (Unison) — hashes identify code.
- Provenance (§27) — hashes form a DAG of who-produced-what.
- Differential dataflow — given the DAG and a delta on inputs,
  propagate the minimum delta on outputs.
- Salsa / Adapton — same primitive, different scope.
- The frontier: **a language where the entire program is in
  continuous incremental re-evaluation across human edits, AI edits,
  sensor updates, and user inputs.** The "rebuild" is incremental by
  construction.

## §39. Bidirectional programming and lenses

The reference points:
- **Boomerang / Augeas** (Foster, Greenwald, Moore, Pierce, Schmitt;
  POPL 2005) — bidirectional transformations on strings; production:
  Augeas for config files.
- **Profunctor optics** (Pickering, Gibbons, Wu; Bartosz Milewski's
  writeup; Bryce Clarke's category-theoretic accounts) — the elegant
  categorical foundation.
- **Edward Kmett's `lens` library** (Haskell) — the practical
  workhorse.
- **Quotient Lenses** (Foster, Pilkiewicz, Pierce; ICFP 2008) —
  lenses that preserve equivalence classes.
- **Symmetric Lenses** (Hofmann, Pierce, Wagner; POPL 2011) —
  symmetric setting.

For SIGIL: bidirectional transformations naturally model *consent
flows.* "User edits this field" and "system applies a transformation
to that field" need to be reconcilable; lenses are the formal
mechanism. Combined with content addressing (§12), every
transformation is a hash-stable bijection, and undo (§21) is
automatic.

## §40. Synthesis-by-example, programming-by-demonstration

The mature options:
- **FlashFill** (Gulwani; POPL 2011) — Excel's flash-fill, by-example
  synthesis on string transformations. Shipped to billions of users.
- **FlashExtract** (Le, Gulwani; PLDI 2014) — generalized to data
  extraction.
- **Sketch** (Solar-Lezama; OOPSLA 2008) — sketch the structure,
  synthesize the holes via CEGIS.
- **Rosette** (Torlak, Bodik; PLDI 2014) — synthesis + verification
  host language, used in Bagpipe, Wallingford, Synapse.
- **SyGuS** (Alur et al., FMCAD 2013) — Syntax-Guided Synthesis
  competition format; standardized.
- **DreamCoder** (Ellis, Wong, Nye, Sablé-Meyer, Morales, Hewitt,
  Cary, Solar-Lezama, Tenenbaum; PLDI 2021) — neural-symbolic
  synthesis with library learning.
- **AlphaCode** (DeepMind, 2022; AlphaCode 2, 2023) — large-scale
  neural code synthesis.
- **Magicoder, Phind, Cody, Aider** — production neural synthesis
  tools.

The agenda the user invites: **specs synthesize programs; we type-
check the spec instead of the implementation.** This is realized in
F* and Dafny *for verification* and in DreamCoder *for synthesis.*
The combination — **AI synthesizes a candidate program that
satisfies a refinement-typed spec, and the SMT-checked refinement is
the contract** — is the practical recipe for AI-fleet partnership.
The human provides the spec; the AI provides the implementation; the
type system is the binding contract.
